Subject: translate fchmodat2(2) with the kernel's semantics

fchmodat2 (Linux 6.6, syscall 452 on every architecture) was unknown to
PRoot: it was neither in the seccomp filter nor in the syscall tables, so it
reached the kernel with the guest's path untranslated. glibc 2.39 and later
implement lchmod(3) and fchmodat(..., AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW) with it. With an
absolute guest path the kernel then looked the name up on the host:

  - libarchive's lchmod of every symlink it extracts failed with ENOENT,
    which it reports as a warning: pacman printed "warning given when
    extracting /usr/lib/... (Can't set permissions to 0777)" for each
    symbolic link in a package, and bsdtar -p does the same;
  - fchmodat(path, mode, AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW) of a regular file, e.g.
    Python's os.chmod(path, follow_symlinks=False), failed with ENOENT and
    changed nothing;
  - had the guest path existed on the host, the host file would have been
    the one changed.

It is now handled at syscall entry. A call with a path is expressed with
calls PRoot already translates, so the kernel never sees it and the result
does not depend on whether the device kernel has it:

  flags 0                       fchmodat(dirfd, translated path, mode)
  AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW, symlink  -EOPNOTSUPP, exactly as Linux returns it
  AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW, other    fchmodat(translated, not followed path)
  AT_EMPTY_PATH with ""         unchanged: there is no path to translate
  any other flag                -EINVAL

The empty-path form goes to the kernel as it is, so it acts on any
descriptor exactly as natively. An earlier version of this patch rewrote it
to fchmod(dirfd), which fails with EBADF on an O_PATH descriptor: on a phone
(Linux 6.12) systemd-tmpfiles' pacman hook, which changes modes through
O_PATH descriptors, failed with "fchmod() of /run/... failed: Bad file
descriptor". A kernel older than 6.6 answers ENOSYS for it, as natively, and
callers fall back to /proc/self/fd.

Nothing is faked: a symlink is never chmodded and never followed, a
regular file gets exactly the requested mode, and every error the kernel
returns for the equivalent fchmodat reaches the tracee. libarchive treats
EOPNOTSUPP as "symlink modes are not supported here", as on any Linux,
so the warning is gone because the operation now behaves like Linux, not
because it is filtered. The symlink decision is made on the translated host
path after extensions resolved it, so a link2symlink "hard link" is the
regular file it stands for. fake_id0 handles fchmodat2 wherever it handles
fchmodat (EPERM emulation for the fake root, mode restoration).

Verified on x86_64 Linux 6.18 with glibc 2.39: lchmod/fchmodat/raw
fchmodat2 results under PRoot are identical to the native kernel's for a
symlink, a regular file, a relative path, AT_EMPTY_PATH on an ordinary and
on an O_PATH descriptor (file, directory, symlink, with and without
AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW) and an invalid flag; a libalpm-style libarchive
extraction no longer warns.

Upstream: termux/proot @ 7266fb3e8516535682f5a9c8f3a7e70f6506eddb (v5.1.107.92), after patches 0001-0005

